name gun-bfm-1
timeout 400

# THE GUN ENGAGEMENT. bfm-basic.fbm's setup, flown to a firing conclusion: VIPER works its way into the
# control position behind BANDIT on radar contacts alone, and this time it is allowed to shoot.
#
# THE DEFENDER FLIES STRAIGHT, and that is the deliberate difference from bfm-basic (whose bandit holds a
# sustained max-rate turn): this is a TRACKING PASS, the setup a gun engagement is flown and measured in.
# What it isolates is the weapon chain — the EEGS solution, the trigger and its interlocks, the rounds'
# ballistics, what they hit and what that does — against a target whose motion is a straight line, so
# every metre of miss distance is the shooter's. Gunning down a defender in a max-rate break is a
# PILOT problem and an open one: measured against bfm-basic's turning bandit this same pilot gets its
# two squeezes off and misses, because its tracking solution sweeps through the funnel faster than the
# rounds' own time of flight (see the report). Nothing about the gun changes between the two.
#
# WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM bfm-basic, and it is exactly three lines on viper:
#   set brief_master_arm arm     the gun is a weapon, so it is behind the master arm and behind the
#                                command bus like every other cockpit action — the pilot ARMS the jet
#                                once airborne, over the bus, and the jet may refuse (it does refuse on
#                                the ground: systems/FBGunSystem's weight-on-wheels interlock).
#   set pilot_bfm_ctrl_min_nm    THE GUNS CONTROL POSITION. The default control band (0.5-1.5 nm) is a
#   set pilot_bfm_ctrl_max_nm    missile-employment position: doc/f16/weapons.md §2.5 puts the EEGS
#                                funnel's own range window at 600-3,000 ft (0.10-0.49 nm), so a pilot
#                                flying the default band is never in gun range at all (measured on
#                                bfm-basic: it settles at 0.655 nm and its closest approach in five
#                                minutes is 0.574 nm). 0.15-0.40 nm puts the same pursuit law's target
#                                position inside the funnel. Nothing else about the fight changes —
#                                same law, same gains, one declared band (systems/FBPilotTuning).
# THE RUN ENDS AT ITS TIMEOUT, exit 3, BY DESIGN — bfm-basic's rule, for bfm-basic's reason: a fight has
# no waypoint to arrive at, and NINE 20 mm rounds through a forward fuselage are not an airframe kill
# (they are what the damage lines below show them to be: the avionics gone and the structure hurt). The
# verdict is read from the events and the telemetry, not from the exit code.
#
# THE DEFENDER is bfm-basic's, unchanged and deliberately not a second gunfighter: a sustained max-rate
# level left turn around a waypoint at the centre of its own turn circle, declared as one mission line
# and flown at the guidance's bank limit for the whole run. It carries NO objectives, so it cannot end
# the run on its own account and the fight runs until viper's objective is met (or the clock).
#
# WHAT THE RUN PRODUCES, all measured, all in events.log + telemetry.csv:
#   pilot GUN_TRACK  the moment the pilot starts flying the funnel instead of the pursuit curve
#   gun TRIGGER      every squeeze the pilot commanded and what was left in the drum
#   gun BURST        every bundle that left the muzzle: rounds, launch velocity, remaining
#   gun HIT          every bundle that passed close enough to matter: expected rounds on the airframe,
#                    miss distance, the pattern's spread there, impact speed, the areal energy, the zone
#   damage SYSTEM    what those rounds wrecked, one line per system
#   damage KILL      the moment bandit stopped being able to fly its sortie
#   gun_* columns    rounds remaining/fired/firing as a time series, both jets
#
# GEOMETRY AT T=0 is bfm-basic's, to the digit: viper 2 nm dead astern, co-altitude, nose on, at the
# model's measured corner speed (380 KCAS, `make test-corner`).

unit viper
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 46.67485 6.85148 4000 90.0 380       # 2 nm in trail, co-altitude, corner speed
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set datalink off                           # no net: the fight is flown on the radar alone
  set fcr_mode acm_hud                       # the 30x20 auto-locking ACM box
  set task bfm
  set brief_master_arm arm                   # entered over the bus once airborne, like every brief item
  set pilot_bfm_ctrl_min_nm 0.15             # the guns control position (see the header)
  set pilot_bfm_ctrl_max_nm 0.40
  # A BRIEFED STEERPOINT, and it is not decoration: the fire-control block publishes only with a nav
  # solution behind it (modules/f16/FBF16FireControl gates the whole block on the nav/platform heads,
  # because in the real jet the FCC is fed by the INS), and the EEGS solution is one of that block's
  # products. The Bfm phase never steers to it — a fight has no waypoint — and it is not judged either,
  # because this unit's `objective` block is the complete statement of what it has to achieve.
  wp 46.70000 6.90000 4000 380

unit bandit
  module f16
  team hostile
  spawn 46.67485 6.90000 4000 90.0 300       # 2 nm ahead of viper, straight and level
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode off                           # the defender is a target, it does not look
  wp 46.67485 7.60000 4000 300               # straight ahead, 50 km east: a tracking pass, see the header
